{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-cf55840a-9b13-4f7e-ae4e-d42481c4e98b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me check the strategy's \"Prerequisites & Process Gates\" — the strategy doesn't have one (it's a synthetic simple strategy; only Problem Statement, HLRs, Affected Components, Out of Scope, Open Questions). The docs-authoring check under Criterion 7: \"if the strategy's Prerequisites & Process Gates section lists Documentation Support: Yes, the epic set must include a docs-authoring epic\". The strategy has no such section. But HLR-4 explicitly requires documentation, and E003 covers it. So docs coverage exists anyway.\n\nNow evaluate each criterion:\n\n**Step 1.5 Triage check:** triage is null → full decomposition, 3 epics fine.\n\n**Step 1.7 Validator:** OK.\n\n**Criterion 1: HLR Coverage**\n- HLR-1 (P0) → E001 ✓\n- HLR-2 (P1) → E002 ✓\n- HLR-3 (P1) → E001, consolidated with HLR-1 per redundant-HLR rule. The consolidation rationale is recorded in the matrix row (\"Consolidated with HLR-1 (redundant/overlapping HLR)\" with full analysis). This matches Rule from decompose prompt (redundant HLRs — consolidate, never split). Note: the review prompt for bundling wants the row \"flagged as bundled with rationale\" — here it's flagged \"Consolidated\" with rationale in the row. The substance is the same — analysis recorded where the mapping is read. This is the redundant-HLR case which the decompose rule explicitly handles (not a priority collapse). I won't flag.\n- HLR-4 (P2) → E003 docs-authoring, exempt from priority inheritance, Major→P1 ✓.\n- Priority inheritance: E001 blocks E002 (P1) and E003. E001 is P0, fine. E002 blocks E003 (docs-authoring, exempt). No inversions.\n- Priority collapse check: E001 maps to P0+P1 (HLR-1, HLR-3). Is this a deferrable distinct feature? Per the redundant-HLR rule, they're the same surface — consolidation is mandated. Rationale recorded in matrix row. Good.\n- Traceability matrix present. Score 2.\n\n**Criterion 2: DAG Coherence**\nEdges: E001→E002 (Rule 13), E001→E003 (Rule 11), E002→E003 (Rule 11). \n\nCheck E001→E002: Rule 13 — implementation producing artifact another epic's code builds against. E002's deliverable includes an affordance in the Model Registry view to open the run comparison. Hmm — is E002's code building against an API/CRD produced by E001? E001 produces a comparison view in the Dashboard. E002's \"open comparison from registry\" builds against the comparison surface. Actually the Model Registry UI is surfaced through the Dashboard (per model-registry.md: \"Dashboard for the registry UI\"). So E002's registry-UI affordance links into the Dashboard's comparison view — a UI surface dependency. Is that a build-against artifact (API/CRD/library)? It's a UI surface/route. Rule 13 says \"artifact another epic's code builds against (API, CRD, library)\". A comparison view deep-link could arguably be a configuration reference (URL). Hmm. But it's more than a digest — the \"open a comparison with the linked run\" flow depends on the comparison view existing with a way to accept a run selection. This is plausibly Rule 13. Alternatively, could it be an AC gate (\"comparison opens with linked run\")? \n\nLet me think about whether this edge is unjustified serialization. E002's core work (recording run link on model version, API retrieval) is independent — the summary acknowledges this. The registry-UI affordance builds against E001's surface. The DAG justification is on record and cites Rule 13 with rationale. Is it a \"major\" unjustified blocking edge? The rule about configuration references applies to image digests/endpoints URLs consumed as content. A UI entry point that opens a comparison pre-populated with a run is building against the comparison view's interface (how to navigate/parameterize it). I'd call it defensible under Rule 13. Also note both epics are in different components/repos (odh-dashboard vs model-r